-- What a string literal costs once nothing has to test where its bytes live.
--
-- A literal's run is `runBorrowed` - the constant says so, `Native/Run.native.yana`'s `releaseRun`
-- tests it, and the test is the whole of `Reclaim(String)`. So the teardown is provably no
-- instructions at all, and for a long time it was emitted anyway.
--
-- **The constant used to have to travel to reach the test, and now it does not.** `stringLiteral`
-- built the value, so the flag was written into a `Run`, copied into an `Array`, copied into a
-- `StringData`, moved into the caller's `String` slot and read back through a `borrow` and a `cast`
-- - four of those five steps being places `opt_place.cpp` stopped carrying what it knew, which is
-- the account in Analysis-Borrows.md §8.9. And it only worked at all where `stringLiteral` was
-- *inlined*, which at 118 call sites it usually was not.
--
-- A literal is now a bitwise duplicate of an immutable global holding the two words (see
-- `resolveString`), and `inheritConstant` reads the flag straight off that constant. Nothing has to
-- inline, nothing has to be forwarded through five steps, and the construction is one block copy
-- instead of three allocations and two aggregate copies.
--
-- `.opt.expect` is what this asserts and it is the point of the fixture: `length(s)` folds to the
-- literal's own count, and after the copy there is **no borrow, no cast, no load, no placement test,
-- no branch and no `freeHeap`**.
--
-- Native only. On JS a literal is a host string and the host collector owns the bytes, so there is
-- no placement flag for any of this to be about.

fn litLen(n: Size) -> Size:
    let s = "hello"
    return length(s) + n

-- The same one level out, so that what is asserted is the fold rather than one function's shape.
fn twice(n: Size) -> Size = litLen(n) + litLen(n + 1)

fn main() -> Int = truncate(twice(3)) :: Int
